[2026-03-04T21:23:40.326Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CLcemsnw8M5rMXcRTeMZ9u","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Skill\n     2→\n     3→You will run all the tests in the app and get them to pass..\n     4→\n     5→## Unpack Subtasks\n     6→\n     7→List all test files in the app's `tests/` directory. Add one task per test file, in reverse\n     8→order (last file first) so they run in alphabetical order. Make sure to call add-task separately\n     9→for each spec file.\n    10→\n    11→```\n    12→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts --skill \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\" --app \"<AppName>\" \\\n    13→  --subtask \"FixTests: tests/<file>.spec.ts\"\n    14→```\n    15→\n    16→After running tests and there are failures, pick specific failing tests and add a task to fix\n    17→them without regressing any tests that passed in previous runs:\n    18→\n    19→```\n    20→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts --skill \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\" --app \"<AppName>\" \\\n    21→  --subtask \"Fix: <failing test name 1>\" \\\n    22→  --subtask \"Fix: <failing test name 2>\"\n    23→```\n    24→\n    25→## Requirements\n    26→\n    27→Once all playwright tests have been written, you must get them to pass.\n    28→When getting tests to pass, you must ensure the app is actually working.\n    29→You must not paper over problems in the app by hard-coding values,\n    30→disabling test functionality, and so on.\n    31→\n    32→You might encounter bugs in the tests and can fix those, but you must ensure\n    33→that the test is correctly exercising the app as described in the corresponding\n    34→entry in tests.md\n    35→\n    36→You MUST read these skill files before testing.\n    37→\n    38→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md\n    39→https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md\n    40→\n    41→RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY is already set in the environment for using the Replay CLI.\n    42→\n    43→## Replay.io Playwright Installation and Configuration\n    44→\n    45→Every app MUST use the Replay browser to record Playwright test runs. This section describes\n    46→the exact installation steps and configuration required.\n    47→\n    48→### 1. Install dependencies\n    49→\n    50→The app's `package.json` must include these dev dependencies:\n    51→\n    52→```bash\n    53→npm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright @playwright/test\n    54→```\n    55→\n    56→`@replayio/playwright` (currently v5.0.1) provides the `replayReporter` and `devices` exports\n    57→for integrating Replay recordings into Playwright.\n    58→\n    59→### 2. Install the Replay browser\n    60→\n    61→Run once in the container (or as part of CI setup):\n    62→\n    63→```bash\n    64→npx replayio install\n    65→```\n    66→\n    67→This downloads the Replay Chromium browser to `~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome`.\n    68→Verify it exists:\n    69→\n    70→```bash\n    71→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n    72→```\n    73→\n    74→### 3. API key\n    75→\n    76→`RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY` must be set in the environment. It is already available in this\n    77→container. The Playwright config passes it to the reporter via:\n    78→\n    79→```ts\n    80→apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY\n    81→```\n    82→\n    83→### 4. Playwright config\n    84→\n    85→The Playwright config MUST:\n    86→\n    87→1. Import BOTH `replayReporter` and `devices as replayDevices` from `@replayio/playwright`.\n    88→2. Include `replayReporter(...)` in the `reporter` array with `upload: false`. Uploads are\n    89→   handled by the test script, NOT by the reporter.\n    90→3. Spread `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` into the global `use` config. This sets\n    91→   `executablePath`, `RECORD_ALL_CONTENT`, and critically `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE` which\n    92→   is required for the reporter to attach test metadata (pass/fail result) to recordings.\n    93→   Without `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`, the recording driver cannot read the per-test\n    94→   metadata written by the fixture, and all recordings will have empty `testResult`.\n    95→\n    96→**IMPORTANT**: Do NOT manually set `launchOptions.executablePath` and `env` instead of using\n    97→`replayDevices['Replay Chromium']`. Manual config misses `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`,\n    98→which breaks the recording-to-test metadata association.\n    99→\n   100→**IMPORTANT**: The Replay Chromium browser adds instrumentation overhead that makes all\n   101→operations ~2–3x slower than standard Chromium. Always set higher timeouts from the start:\n   102→\n   103→- `actionTimeout: 15000` (15s for clicks, fills, etc.)\n   104→- `navigationTimeout: 30000` (30s for page navigations)\n   105→- Use `expect.toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 })` for post-navigation assertions\n   106→\n   107→Example:\n   108→\n   109→```ts\n   110→import { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\n   111→import { devices as replayDevices, replayReporter } from '@replayio/playwright';\n   112→\n   113→export default defineConfig({\n   114→  // Replay Chromium is slower due to instrumentation — use generous timeouts\n   115→  actionTimeout: 15000,\n   116→  navigationTimeout: 30000,\n   117→  reporter: [\n   118→    replayReporter({\n   119→      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY,\n   120→      upload: false,\n   121→    }),\n   122→    ['json', { outputFile: 'test-results/results.json' }],\n   123→    ['list'],\n   124→  ],\n   125→  use: {\n   126→    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n   127→  },\n   128→});\n   129→```\n   130→\n   131→### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n   132→\n   133→- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n   134→- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n   135→  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n   136→  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n   137→- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n   138→- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n   139→- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n   140→  data accumulating).\n   141→\n   142→### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n   143→\n   144→The Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\n   145→container only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\n   146→test output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n   147→`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\n   148→the deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n   149→\n   150→### 7. Pre-test verification\n   151→\n   152→Before running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n   153→\n   154→```bash\n   155→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n   156→```\n   157→\n   158→If it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\n   159→without the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\n   160→Do not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n   161→\n   162→## Pre-Flight Checks\n   163→\n   164→Before running any tests, perform these checks from the app directory:\n   165→\n   166→1. **Verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` is set**: `grep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env` — the test script requires\n   167→   it for creating ephemeral Neon branches. If missing, check `deployment.txt` for the project ID.\n   168→2. **Kill stale servers**: `pkill -f \"netlify dev\" 2>/dev/null; pkill -f \"vite\" 2>/dev/null`\n   169→3. **Verify dependencies**: `ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install`\n   170→\n   171→See `skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for full environment prerequisites.\n   172→\n   173→## Running Tests\n   174→\n   175→Run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` from the app directory (see `skills/scripts/test.md`\n   176→for the full script specification). Do NOT manually run playwright or start dev servers.\n   177→\n   178→- The script prints a one-line summary to stdout (e.g., `8 passed, 2 failed — see logs/test-run-3.log`).\n   179→- Full test output and recording metadata are in the log file referenced by the summary.\n   180→- Read the log file only when you need to diagnose failures.\n   181→- Tests MUST run in parallel with multiple workers (use `fullyParallel: true` in playwright config).\n   182→\n   183→## Debugging\n   184→\n   185→When tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\n   186→mandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n   187→\n   188→1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n   189→2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   190→   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   191→   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   192→   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   193→   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   194→   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   195→   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   196→   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   197→   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n   198→3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   199→   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   200→   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   201→   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   202→   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   203→   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n   204→4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n   205→5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   206→   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   207→   making any changes.\n   208→6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   209→   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n   210→7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   211→   on what you found.\n   212→8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   213→   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   214→   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n   215→\n   216→### Bug Writeup Template\n   217→\n   218→For each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n   219→\n   220→```\n   221→# Bug: <test name>\n   222→\n   223→## Step 1: Evidence\n   224→\n   225→Evidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\n   226→responses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\n   227→no evidence.>\n   228→\n   229→Evidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\n   230→selectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n   231→\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n   232→\n   233→## Step 2: Determination\n   234→\n   235→Which is broken: <APP or TEST>\n   236→\n   237→## Step 3: Root Cause\n   238→\n   239→<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\n   240→function(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n   241→```\n   242→\n   243→Do NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\n   244→Do NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\n   245→Do NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\n   246→The Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n   247→\n   248→When testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n   249→\n   250→## Directives\n   251→\n   252→- Do NOT manually start `netlify dev` for testing. The test script manages the dev server\n   253→  automatically. Only use manual `netlify dev` for one-off curl checks.\n   254→\n   255→- If you do manually start `netlify dev` for curl testing, you MUST pass `--functions ./netlify/functions`\n   256→  to avoid 404 errors on function endpoints. The `base` setting in `netlify.toml` causes the functions\n   257→  directory to resolve incorrectly without this flag. Example:\n   258→  `npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n   259→\n   260→- The Playwright `webServer` command should also include `--functions ./netlify/functions` when\n   261→  using Netlify Functions, to ensure function endpoints resolve correctly during test runs.\n   262→  Example: `npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n   263→\n   264→- After making code changes to Netlify Functions or frontend code during a testing session, ALWAYS\n   265→  restart the dev server before re-running tests. The dev server may cache old function bundles.\n   266→  Kill background processes with `pkill -f \"netlify\"` and `pkill -f \"vite\"` before restarting.\n   267→\n   268→- All browsers must run headless. Never use Xvfb, never set `DISPLAY`, never use the `replayio record`\n   269→  CLI (it launches a headed browser). Use `@replayio/playwright` for recordings.\n   270→\n   271→- Tests run serially with `--workers 1`. The test script resets the database between each test.\n   272→\n   273→- Tests MUST NOT run against the production database or the main Neon branch. The test script\n   274→  creates ephemeral Neon branches for test runs and deletes them afterwards.\n   275→\n   276→- NEVER stop, cancel, or skip a Replay recording upload that is in progress. The upload is a\n   277→  prerequisite for Replay MCP analysis, which is mandatory for debugging failures. If the upload\n   278→  is taking a long time, WAIT for it to finish. Do NOT proceed with fixes based on \"enough\n   279→  information\" — you must complete the full upload → MCP analysis → fix cycle. Stopping an\n   280→  upload to \"save time\" violates the debugging process and leads to guesswork-based fixes.\n   281→\n   282→- Always run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` (from the app directory), never by calling\n   283→  `npx playwright test` directly. The test script includes essential pre-test setup (branch\n   284→  creation, schema init, seeding, stale cleanup) that is skipped when running Playwright directly.\n   285→\n   286→## Tips\n   287→\n   288→- When a test times out inside a `.toPass()` block, use `mcp__replay__PlaywrightSteps` first. It\n   289→  shows the exact sequence of Playwright actions with timestamps and return values. Look for a\n   290→  step that is stuck auto-waiting for an element — this reveals nested-wait deadlocks where the\n   291→  DOM changed mid-iteration and an inner `textContent()` or `count()` call is waiting on an\n   292→  element that was removed. The fix is always in the test: replace the iteration loop with a\n   293→  single atomic Playwright assertion (see the nested-wait directive in writeTests.md).\n   294→- When debugging history/timeline tests, check for duplicate entries caused by React re-renders triggering multiple API calls, and check for missing entries from mutation handlers that skip history writes.\n   295→- When many detail-page tests fail with \"expected count > 0, received 0\", the root cause is almost\n   296→  always that the database has no seed data OR the API functions are not working. Check both before\n   297→  attempting to fix individual tests.\n   298→- When filter/search tests return wrong data (e.g., filtering by \"proposal\" returns \"Qualification\"),\n   299→  the root cause may be that the SQL query is silently broken. Verify the API returns correctly\n   300→  filtered results by curling the endpoint directly with filter parameters.\n   301→- Tests that pass individually but fail in the full suite usually indicate data contamination between\n   302→  tests. The test script resets the database between each test, so contamination should not occur.\n   303→  If it does, check that the reset is working correctly.\n   304→- When test IDs differ between spec files (cross-cutting vs page-specific), decide on ONE canonical\n   305→  set of IDs in the component and update whichever test file has fewer references.\n   306→- When testing unauthenticated scenarios with `browser.newContext()`, always pass\n   307→  `storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] }` to ensure a truly empty context. Without this,\n   308→  Supabase or other auth libraries may detect cached sessions from previous test runs, causing\n   309→  \"unauthenticated\" tests to appear authenticated.\n   310→- If many tests suddenly fail with `net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED` or `ERR_CONNECTION_RESET` partway\n   311→  through a test run, the dev server crashed mid-suite. Do NOT debug individual test failures —\n   312→  they are all caused by the same server crash. Re-run the full suite. If the crash recurs, check\n   313→  for memory pressure or port conflicts from zombie processes.\n   314→- Zombie processes (`<defunct>`) from previous `vite` or `netlify` runs are harmless and cannot be\n   315→  killed (they are orphaned child processes waiting for PID 1 to reap them). Ignore them. The\n   316→  Playwright `webServer` config starts fresh processes on different ports and is not affected.\n   317→- Commit incrementally during debugging sessions. If you improve test results (e.g., 81→207 passing)\n   318→  or fix a meaningful issue, commit those changes immediately even if other failures remain. An\n   319→  iteration that produces zero commits despite significant work (skill updates, code fixes,\n   320→  debugging progress) is wasted effort because the next iteration starts from scratch.\n   321→- Before running deployment tests, verify that required environment variables (DATABASE_URL,\n   322→  etc.) are set on the deployment target (e.g., Netlify). Missing env vars cause infrastructure\n   323→  failures that waste a full test cycle. Use `netlify env:list` to check.\n   324→- Before running tests, verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` is set in the environment. The test script\n   325→  requires it for creating ephemeral Neon branches.\n   326→- Running the full test suite at once (e.g., `npx playwright test` with no file argument) can\n   327→  OOM or crash with `ERR_STRING_TOO_LONG` on large suites. Always run tests one spec file at a\n   328→  time using `npm run test tests/<file>.spec.ts` (which uses the test script that manages\n   329→  database branches). When verifying broad changes, pick the most relevant 2-3 spec files.\n   330→- The database is reset between each test, so tests do not need to worry about data from\n   331→  previous tests. Each test starts with a fresh seed.\n   332→- The Vite dev server cold start can cause the first test in a suite to timeout. Consider adding\n   333→  a warm-up navigation in `beforeAll` or increasing the first test's timeout to account for\n   334→  cold start latency.\n   335→- Avoid hardcoding expected values in assertions. Tests that hardcode specific counts (e.g.,\n   336→  `expect 1 remaining task`) or specific names (e.g., `\"David Lee\"`) break when test data\n   337→  changes. Use relative assertions or query actual seed data to derive expected values.\n   338→- Validate that seed data exists before asserting on it. If a test expects a specific assignee\n   339→  name or record count, verify the data is present first. This catches seed data mismatches\n   340→  early instead of producing confusing assertion failures.\n   341→- Before writing tests that rely on `data-testid` attributes, verify those attributes exist in\n   342→  the component source. Missing `data-testid` attributes cause test failures that are easy to\n   343→  prevent with a quick source check.\n   344→- When seed data uses `NUMERIC` or `DECIMAL` database columns, expect formatted output in\n   345→  assertions (e.g., `\"500\"` not `\"500.00\"`). These column types return decimal strings from\n   346→  PostgreSQL, so the app must format them before display. If tests fail on numeric values,\n   347→  check the column type and app formatting layer before modifying test expectations.\n   348→- When navigation timeouts persist across retries for a specific app (e.g., all supplier-details\n   349→  pages timing out), this is likely a Replay browser resource issue rather than an app bug.\n   350→  Adding a retry-with-delay (e.g., 5s between retries) can help distinguish transient\n   351→  infrastructure issues from persistent ones. If retries still fail, skip those tests and\n   352→  note the infrastructure issue.\n   353→- Seed data should use relative dates (e.g., \"current month minus 1\") rather than hardcoded\n   354→  month names. Tests that assert on date-filtered data (e.g., expecting \"Jan\" entries) will\n   355→  fail when run in a different month. Either make seed data date-relative or make test\n   356→  assertions date-aware.\n   357→- When many tests are pre-existing failures unrelated to the current task, avoid re-verifying\n   358→  them on every run. Use the `git stash` triage approach (see `skills/debugging/README.md`)\n   359→  once per task to confirm, then focus on new failures only.\n   360→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4d29cffe-b9f8-4435-a315-855c844adbb1","uuid":"3ab60d5c-52af-4b0a-bdb7-e2096f72c9a7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","content":"# Skill\n\nYou will run all the tests in the app and get them to pass..\n\n## Unpack Subtasks\n\nList all test files in the app's `tests/` directory. Add one task per test file, in reverse\norder (last file first) so they run in alphabetical order. Make sure to call add-task separately\nfor each spec file.\n\n```\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts --skill \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\" --app \"<AppName>\" \\\n  --subtask \"FixTests: tests/<file>.spec.ts\"\n```\n\nAfter running tests and there are failures, pick specific failing tests and add a task to fix\nthem without regressing any tests that passed in previous runs:\n\n```\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts --skill \"skills/tasks/build/testing.md\" --app \"<AppName>\" \\\n  --subtask \"Fix: <failing test name 1>\" \\\n  --subtask \"Fix: <failing test name 2>\"\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nOnce all playwright tests have been written, you must get them to pass.\nWhen getting tests to pass, you must ensure the app is actually working.\nYou must not paper over problems in the app by hard-coding values,\ndisabling test functionality, and so on.\n\nYou might encounter bugs in the tests and can fix those, but you must ensure\nthat the test is correctly exercising the app as described in the corresponding\nentry in tests.md\n\nYou MUST read these skill files before testing.\n\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md\n\nRECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY is already set in the environment for using the Replay CLI.\n\n## Replay.io Playwright Installation and Configuration\n\nEvery app MUST use the Replay browser to record Playwright test runs. This section describes\nthe exact installation steps and configuration required.\n\n### 1. Install dependencies\n\nThe app's `package.json` must include these dev dependencies:\n\n```bash\nnpm install --save-dev @replayio/playwright @playwright/test\n```\n\n`@replayio/playwright` (currently v5.0.1) provides the `replayReporter` and `devices` exports\nfor integrating Replay recordings into Playwright.\n\n### 2. Install the Replay browser\n\nRun once in the container (or as part of CI setup):\n\n```bash\nnpx replayio install\n```\n\nThis downloads the Replay Chromium browser to `~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome`.\nVerify it exists:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n```\n\n### 3. API key\n\n`RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY` must be set in the environment. It is already available in this\ncontainer. The Playwright config passes it to the reporter via:\n\n```ts\napiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY\n```\n\n### 4. Playwright config\n\nThe Playwright config MUST:\n\n1. Import BOTH `replayReporter` and `devices as replayDevices` from `@replayio/playwright`.\n2. Include `replayReporter(...)` in the `reporter` array with `upload: false`. Uploads are\n   handled by the test script, NOT by the reporter.\n3. Spread `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` into the global `use` config. This sets\n   `executablePath`, `RECORD_ALL_CONTENT`, and critically `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE` which\n   is required for the reporter to attach test metadata (pass/fail result) to recordings.\n   Without `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`, the recording driver cannot read the per-test\n   metadata written by the fixture, and all recordings will have empty `testResult`.\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Do NOT manually set `launchOptions.executablePath` and `env` instead of using\n`replayDevices['Replay Chromium']`. Manual config misses `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`,\nwhich breaks the recording-to-test metadata association.\n\n**IMPORTANT**: The Replay Chromium browser adds instrumentation overhead that makes all\noperations ~2–3x slower than standard Chromium. Always set higher timeouts from the start:\n\n- `actionTimeout: 15000` (15s for clicks, fills, etc.)\n- `navigationTimeout: 30000` (30s for page navigations)\n- Use `expect.toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 })` for post-navigation assertions\n\nExample:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\nimport { devices as replayDevices, replayReporter } from '@replayio/playwright';\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  // Replay Chromium is slower due to instrumentation — use generous timeouts\n  actionTimeout: 15000,\n  navigationTimeout: 30000,\n  reporter: [\n    replayReporter({\n      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY,\n      upload: false,\n    }),\n    ['json', { outputFile: 'test-results/results.json' }],\n    ['list'],\n  ],\n  use: {\n    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n  },\n});\n```\n\n### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n\n- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n  data accumulating).\n\n### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n\nThe Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\ncontainer only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\ntest output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\nthe deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n\n### 7. Pre-test verification\n\nBefore running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n```\n\nIf it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\nwithout the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\nDo not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n\n## Pre-Flight Checks\n\nBefore running any tests, perform these checks from the app directory:\n\n1. **Verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` is set**: `grep NEON_PROJECT_ID .env` — the test script requires\n   it for creating ephemeral Neon branches. If missing, check `deployment.txt` for the project ID.\n2. **Kill stale servers**: `pkill -f \"netlify dev\" 2>/dev/null; pkill -f \"vite\" 2>/dev/null`\n3. **Verify dependencies**: `ls node_modules/@neondatabase/serverless 2>/dev/null || npm install`\n\nSee `skills/scripts/env-setup.md` for full environment prerequisites.\n\n## Running Tests\n\nRun tests via `npm run test <testFile>` from the app directory (see `skills/scripts/test.md`\nfor the full script specification). Do NOT manually run playwright or start dev servers.\n\n- The script prints a one-line summary to stdout (e.g., `8 passed, 2 failed — see logs/test-run-3.log`).\n- Full test output and recording metadata are in the log file referenced by the summary.\n- Read the log file only when you need to diagnose failures.\n- Tests MUST run in parallel with multiple workers (use `fullyParallel: true` in playwright config).\n\n## Debugging\n\nWhen tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\nmandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n\n1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   making any changes.\n6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   on what you found.\n8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n\n### Bug Writeup Template\n\nFor each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n\n```\n# Bug: <test name>\n\n## Step 1: Evidence\n\nEvidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\nresponses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\nno evidence.>\n\nEvidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\nselectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n\n## Step 2: Determination\n\nWhich is broken: <APP or TEST>\n\n## Step 3: Root Cause\n\n<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\nfunction(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n```\n\nDo NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\nDo NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\nDo NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\nThe Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n\nWhen testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n\n## Directives\n\n- Do NOT manually start `netlify dev` for testing. The test script manages the dev server\n  automatically. Only use manual `netlify dev` for one-off curl checks.\n\n- If you do manually start `netlify dev` for curl testing, you MUST pass `--functions ./netlify/functions`\n  to avoid 404 errors on function endpoints. The `base` setting in `netlify.toml` causes the functions\n  directory to resolve incorrectly without this flag. Example:\n  `npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n\n- The Playwright `webServer` command should also include `--functions ./netlify/functions` when\n  using Netlify Functions, to ensure function endpoints resolve correctly during test runs.\n  Example: `npx netlify dev --port 8888 --functions ./netlify/functions`\n\n- After making code changes to Netlify Functions or frontend code during a testing session, ALWAYS\n  restart the dev server before re-running tests. The dev server may cache old function bundles.\n  Kill background processes with `pkill -f \"netlify\"` and `pkill -f \"vite\"` before restarting.\n\n- All browsers must run headless. Never use Xvfb, never set `DISPLAY`, never use the `replayio record`\n  CLI (it launches a headed browser). Use `@replayio/playwright` for recordings.\n\n- Tests run serially with `--workers 1`. The test script resets the database between each test.\n\n- Tests MUST NOT run against the production database or the main Neon branch. The test script\n  creates ephemeral Neon branches for test runs and deletes them afterwards.\n\n- NEVER stop, cancel, or skip a Replay recording upload that is in progress. The upload is a\n  prerequisite for Replay MCP analysis, which is mandatory for debugging failures. If the upload\n  is taking a long time, WAIT for it to finish. Do NOT proceed with fixes based on \"enough\n  information\" — you must complete the full upload → MCP analysis → fix cycle. Stopping an\n  upload to \"save time\" violates the debugging process and leads to guesswork-based fixes.\n\n- Always run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` (from the app directory), never by calling\n  `npx playwright test` directly. The test script includes essential pre-test setup (branch\n  creation, schema init, seeding, stale cleanup) that is skipped when running Playwright directly.\n\n## Tips\n\n- When a test times out inside a `.toPass()` block, use `mcp__replay__PlaywrightSteps` first. It\n  shows the exact sequence of Playwright actions with timestamps and return values. Look for a\n  step that is stuck auto-waiting for an element — this reveals nested-wait deadlocks where the\n  DOM changed mid-iteration and an inner `textContent()` or `count()` call is waiting on an\n  element that was removed. The fix is always in the test: replace the iteration loop with a\n  single atomic Playwright assertion (see the nested-wait directive in writeTests.md).\n- When debugging history/timeline tests, check for duplicate entries caused by React re-renders triggering multiple API calls, and check for missing entries from mutation handlers that skip history writes.\n- When many detail-page tests fail with \"expected count > 0, received 0\", the root cause is almost\n  always that the database has no seed data OR the API functions are not working. Check both before\n  attempting to fix individual tests.\n- When filter/search tests return wrong data (e.g., filtering by \"proposal\" returns \"Qualification\"),\n  the root cause may be that the SQL query is silently broken. Verify the API returns correctly\n  filtered results by curling the endpoint directly with filter parameters.\n- Tests that pass individually but fail in the full suite usually indicate data contamination between\n  tests. The test script resets the database between each test, so contamination should not occur.\n  If it does, check that the reset is working correctly.\n- When test IDs differ between spec files (cross-cutting vs page-specific), decide on ONE canonical\n  set of IDs in the component and update whichever test file has fewer references.\n- When testing unauthenticated scenarios with `browser.newContext()`, always pass\n  `storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] }` to ensure a truly empty context. Without this,\n  Supabase or other auth libraries may detect cached sessions from previous test runs, causing\n  \"unauthenticated\" tests to appear authenticated.\n- If many tests suddenly fail with `net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED` or `ERR_CONNECTION_RESET` partway\n  through a test run, the dev server crashed mid-suite. Do NOT debug individual test failures —\n  they are all caused by the same server crash. Re-run the full suite. If the crash recurs, check\n  for memory pressure or port conflicts from zombie processes.\n- Zombie processes (`<defunct>`) from previous `vite` or `netlify` runs are harmless and cannot be\n  killed (they are orphaned child processes waiting for PID 1 to reap them). Ignore them. The\n  Playwright `webServer` config starts fresh processes on different ports and is not affected.\n- Commit incrementally during debugging sessions. If you improve test results (e.g., 81→207 passing)\n  or fix a meaningful issue, commit those changes immediately even if other failures remain. An\n  iteration that produces zero commits despite significant work (skill updates, code fixes,\n  debugging progress) is wasted effort because the next iteration starts from scratch.\n- Before running deployment tests, verify that required environment variables (DATABASE_URL,\n  etc.) are set on the deployment target (e.g., Netlify). Missing env vars cause infrastructure\n  failures that waste a full test cycle. Use `netlify env:list` to check.\n- Before running tests, verify `NEON_PROJECT_ID` is set in the environment. The test script\n  requires it for creating ephemeral Neon branches.\n- Running the full test suite at once (e.g., `npx playwright test` with no file argument) can\n  OOM or crash with `ERR_STRING_TOO_LONG` on large suites. Always run tests one spec file at a\n  time using `npm run test tests/<file>.spec.ts` (which uses the test script that manages\n  database branches). When verifying broad changes, pick the most relevant 2-3 spec files.\n- The database is reset between each test, so tests do not need to worry about data from\n  previous tests. Each test starts with a fresh seed.\n- The Vite dev server cold start can cause the first test in a suite to timeout. Consider adding\n  a warm-up navigation in `beforeAll` or increasing the first test's timeout to account for\n  cold start latency.\n- Avoid hardcoding expected values in assertions. Tests that hardcode specific counts (e.g.,\n  `expect 1 remaining task`) or specific names (e.g., `\"David Lee\"`) break when test data\n  changes. Use relative assertions or query actual seed data to derive expected values.\n- Validate that seed data exists before asserting on it. If a test expects a specific assignee\n  name or record count, verify the data is present first. This catches seed data mismatches\n  early instead of producing confusing assertion failures.\n- Before writing tests that rely on `data-testid` attributes, verify those attributes exist in\n  the component source. Missing `data-testid` attributes cause test failures that are easy to\n  prevent with a quick source check.\n- When seed data uses `NUMERIC` or `DECIMAL` database columns, expect formatted output in\n  assertions (e.g., `\"500\"` not `\"500.00\"`). These column types return decimal strings from\n  PostgreSQL, so the app must format them before display. If tests fail on numeric values,\n  check the column type and app formatting layer before modifying test expectations.\n- When navigation timeouts persist across retries for a specific app (e.g., all supplier-details\n  pages timing out), this is likely a Replay browser resource issue rather than an app bug.\n  Adding a retry-with-delay (e.g., 5s between retries) can help distinguish transient\n  infrastructure issues from persistent ones. If retries still fail, skip those tests and\n  note the infrastructure issue.\n- Seed data should use relative dates (e.g., \"current month minus 1\") rather than hardcoded\n  month names. Tests that assert on date-filtered data (e.g., expecting \"Jan\" entries) will\n  fail when run in a different month. Either make seed data date-relative or make test\n  assertions date-aware.\n- When many tests are pre-existing failures unrelated to the current task, avoid re-verifying\n  them on every run. Use the `git stash` triage approach (see `skills/debugging/README.md`)\n  once per task to confirm, then focus on new failures only.\n","numLines":360,"startLine":1,"totalLines":360}}}
[2026-03-04T21:23:40.332Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L8NRKwfwUoPPJhC1bQG71s","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Overview\n     2→\n     3→This repository is a platform for agentic app building. You will build and/or maintain one or more\n     4→web apps according to a set of skills and guidelines which will evolve as you get better at building apps.\n     5→\n     6→Key directories:\n     7→\n     8→* `apps`: Has one subdirectory for each app that has been built or has been specified and still needs to be built.\n     9→* `skills/messages`: Skills for responding to messages from the user (e.g. bug reports, log analysis).\n    10→* `skills/tasks`: Skills for performing tasks. See `skills/AGENTS.md` for details.\n    11→* `reports`: Final generated reports from log analysis (see `skills/review/`).\n    12→* `report-data`: Intermediate analysis data used during report generation.\n    13→* `tasks`: The task queue (`tasks-<containerName>.json`) managed by scripts in `scripts/`.\n    14→* `logs`: Log files from work that has been performed. `worker-current.log` is the log for\n    15→  the work currently being done.\n    16→\n    17→You are running within a container and can run test suites for applications and connect to various\n    18→external services using instructions in the relevant skill files.\n    19→\n    20→**CRITICAL — FIRST THING YOU DO when responding to a user message:**\n    21→\n    22→1. **BEFORE doing ANY work**, list the files in `skills/messages/` and read any skill\n    23→   whose name matches the user's request.\n    24→2. If a matching skill exists, follow it exactly.\n    25→3. Only if no matching skill exists should you proceed on your own — and then write a new\n    26→   skill document in `skills/messages/` afterward so you handle it correctly next time.\n    27→\n    28→Do NOT run git commands, search code, or take any other action until you have checked for a\n    29→matching skill. This is the highest-priority rule when handling user messages.\n    30→\n    31→Do NOT modify this AGENTS.md file unless the user explicitly instructs you to.\n    32→All domain-specific guidance belongs in skill files under `skills/`.\n    33→\n    34→## Worker Execution\n    35→\n    36→The worker processes tasks from `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json` sequentially. For each task,\n    37→it passes all subtasks to Claude as a single prompt and checks for a `<DONE>` signal.\n    38→\n    39→- If `<DONE>` is signaled: the task is dequeued and the worker moves to the next task.\n    40→- If `<DONE>` is NOT signaled: the task is retried (up to 3 times before being skipped).\n    41→\n    42→The worker commits changes after each task automatically. Focus on completing the\n    43→subtasks — do not worry about committing or exiting.\n    44→\n    45→When you have completed ALL subtasks in your task, output `<DONE>` to signal completion.\n    46→\n    47→## Task System\n    48→\n    49→Work is managed through a JSON task queue at `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json`. The file contains an object\n    50→with a `tasks` array. Each task has a `skill`, an array of `subtasks` (description strings),\n    51→a `timestamp`, and an optional `app` name:\n    52→\n    53→```json\n    54→{\n    55→  \"tasks\": [\n    56→    {\n    57→      \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/checkDirectives.md\",\n    58→      \"subtasks\": [\n    59→        \"CheckTestSpecAuth: Check testSpec.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n    60→        \"CheckComponentsAuth: Check writeApp.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n    61→        \"CheckTestsAuth: Check writeTests.md directive violations in Authentication\"\n    62→      ],\n    63→      \"timestamp\": \"2026-02-20T00:00:00.000Z\",\n    64→      \"app\": \"sales-crm\"\n    65→    }\n    66→  ]\n    67→}\n    68→```\n    69→\n    70→The agent NEVER reads or writes task files directly. Instead, use:\n    71→\n    72→* **`npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts --skill \"<path>\" --subtask \"desc1\" --subtask \"desc2\" [--app \"<name>\"]`**:\n    73→  Adds a task to the FRONT of the queue (next to be processed). Each `--subtask` flag\n    74→  adds one subtask to the task. Subtasks execute in the order listed.\n    75→  Use `--app` to associate the task with a specific app (the directory name under `apps/`).\n    76→\n    77→All subtasks in a task share the same skill. Group related subtasks together — for example,\n    78→all checks for a single page go in one task. When a skill needs to \"unpack\" into\n    79→sub-tasks, use `add-task` to insert them at the front of the queue.\n    80→\n    81→**CRITICAL: Task scope rules**\n    82→\n    83→- You MUST only work on subtasks that were assigned to you in your current prompt. Your assigned\n    84→  subtasks are the ones passed to you by the worker — nothing else.\n    85→- When you add new tasks via `add-task`, you MUST NOT start working on those tasks.\n    86→  They will be picked up by a future worker iteration.\n    87→- If you have completed all of your assigned subtasks and the only remaining work is tasks you\n    88→  added to the queue, you are DONE. Commit your changes and output `<DONE>` immediately.\n    89→  Do NOT continue working on the newly queued tasks.\n    90→- Adding tasks to the queue is NOT the same as doing those tasks. Add them and stop.\n    91→\n    92→## Tech Stack\n    93→\n    94→- **Vite** for build tooling\n    95→- **TypeScript** for all source code (strict mode)\n    96→- **React 18** for UI (single-page application)\n    97→- **Redux** for client side state management\n    98→- **shadcn/ui** for components\n    99→- **Neon** for database backend (Postgres).\n   100→- **Netlify Functions** for any backend/serverless functions. Place functions in `netlify/functions/`. Use `netlify dev` for local development.\n   101→- **File Storage** use UploadThing with the provided token for any file storage.\n   102→\n   103→## Database\n   104→\n   105→All database accesses must happen in backend Netlify functions. Netlify functions should be focused\n   106→and operate on specific parts of the database corresponding to the needs of one or more specific\n   107→frontend components.\n   108→\n   109→## Quality Gates\n   110→\n   111→Before each commit, run `npm run check` (see `skills/scripts/check.md`). Do not commit\n   112→code that fails typecheck or lint.\n   113→\n   114→## Commits\n   115→\n   116→The worker commits automatically after each iteration. Do not commit manually unless\n   117→explicitly asked to by a skill or prompt.\n   118→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4d29cffe-b9f8-4435-a315-855c844adbb1","uuid":"73bfbba1-d0ff-4b72-8b7b-989291eed66f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/AGENTS.md","content":"# Overview\n\nThis repository is a platform for agentic app building. You will build and/or maintain one or more\nweb apps according to a set of skills and guidelines which will evolve as you get better at building apps.\n\nKey directories:\n\n* `apps`: Has one subdirectory for each app that has been built or has been specified and still needs to be built.\n* `skills/messages`: Skills for responding to messages from the user (e.g. bug reports, log analysis).\n* `skills/tasks`: Skills for performing tasks. See `skills/AGENTS.md` for details.\n* `reports`: Final generated reports from log analysis (see `skills/review/`).\n* `report-data`: Intermediate analysis data used during report generation.\n* `tasks`: The task queue (`tasks-<containerName>.json`) managed by scripts in `scripts/`.\n* `logs`: Log files from work that has been performed. `worker-current.log` is the log for\n  the work currently being done.\n\nYou are running within a container and can run test suites for applications and connect to various\nexternal services using instructions in the relevant skill files.\n\n**CRITICAL — FIRST THING YOU DO when responding to a user message:**\n\n1. **BEFORE doing ANY work**, list the files in `skills/messages/` and read any skill\n   whose name matches the user's request.\n2. If a matching skill exists, follow it exactly.\n3. Only if no matching skill exists should you proceed on your own — and then write a new\n   skill document in `skills/messages/` afterward so you handle it correctly next time.\n\nDo NOT run git commands, search code, or take any other action until you have checked for a\nmatching skill. This is the highest-priority rule when handling user messages.\n\nDo NOT modify this AGENTS.md file unless the user explicitly instructs you to.\nAll domain-specific guidance belongs in skill files under `skills/`.\n\n## Worker Execution\n\nThe worker processes tasks from `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json` sequentially. For each task,\nit passes all subtasks to Claude as a single prompt and checks for a `<DONE>` signal.\n\n- If `<DONE>` is signaled: the task is dequeued and the worker moves to the next task.\n- If `<DONE>` is NOT signaled: the task is retried (up to 3 times before being skipped).\n\nThe worker commits changes after each task automatically. Focus on completing the\nsubtasks — do not worry about committing or exiting.\n\nWhen you have completed ALL subtasks in your task, output `<DONE>` to signal completion.\n\n## Task System\n\nWork is managed through a JSON task queue at `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json`. The file contains an object\nwith a `tasks` array. Each task has a `skill`, an array of `subtasks` (description strings),\na `timestamp`, and an optional `app` name:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tasks\": [\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/checkDirectives.md\",\n      \"subtasks\": [\n        \"CheckTestSpecAuth: Check testSpec.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n        \"CheckComponentsAuth: Check writeApp.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n        \"CheckTestsAuth: Check writeTests.md directive violations in Authentication\"\n      ],\n      \"timestamp\": \"2026-02-20T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"app\": \"sales-crm\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe agent NEVER reads or writes task files directly. Instead, use:\n\n* **`npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts --skill \"<path>\" --subtask \"desc1\" --subtask \"desc2\" [--app \"<name>\"]`**:\n  Adds a task to the FRONT of the queue (next to be processed). Each `--subtask` flag\n  adds one subtask to the task. Subtasks execute in the order listed.\n  Use `--app` to associate the task with a specific app (the directory name under `apps/`).\n\nAll subtasks in a task share the same skill. Group related subtasks together — for example,\nall checks for a single page go in one task. When a skill needs to \"unpack\" into\nsub-tasks, use `add-task` to insert them at the front of the queue.\n\n**CRITICAL: Task scope rules**\n\n- You MUST only work on subtasks that were assigned to you in your current prompt. Your assigned\n  subtasks are the ones passed to you by the worker — nothing else.\n- When you add new tasks via `add-task`, you MUST NOT start working on those tasks.\n  They will be picked up by a future worker iteration.\n- If you have completed all of your assigned subtasks and the only remaining work is tasks you\n  added to the queue, you are DONE. Commit your changes and output `<DONE>` immediately.\n  Do NOT continue working on the newly queued tasks.\n- Adding tasks to the queue is NOT the same as doing those tasks. Add them and stop.\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n- **Vite** for build tooling\n- **TypeScript** for all source code (strict mode)\n- **React 18** for UI (single-page application)\n- **Redux** for client side state management\n- **shadcn/ui** for components\n- **Neon** for database backend (Postgres).\n- **Netlify Functions** for any backend/serverless functions. Place functions in `netlify/functions/`. Use `netlify dev` for local development.\n- **File Storage** use UploadThing with the provided token for any file storage.\n\n## Database\n\nAll database accesses must happen in backend Netlify functions. Netlify functions should be focused\nand operate on specific parts of the database corresponding to the needs of one or more specific\nfrontend components.\n\n## Quality Gates\n\nBefore each commit, run `npm run check` (see `skills/scripts/check.md`). Do not commit\ncode that fails typecheck or lint.\n\n## Commits\n\nThe worker commits automatically after each iteration. Do not commit manually unless\nexplicitly asked to by a skill or prompt.\n","numLines":118,"startLine":1,"totalLines":118}}}
